[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read report by report, it is
not one issue. Its reports share a subject line, because the desk merges on subject alone
([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), but they come from three different
problems. The queued parser fix closes only the first one.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one bug. Each problem below now has its own ticket, with the reports it covers.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} issues={ISSUES} />

Every count on this page is computed from the ticket export at render time (`#lib/kb/split.ts`),
so it follows the queue as the desk keeps merging.

## The problems

### `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

A tenant that writes the day first types 7 April as `07/04`; the shared parser reads it as 4 July.
Every channel of a day-first tenant hits it, except a booking-v3 form, which reads the tenant's
way. This is the bug #4471 describes, and the queued fix is for this one.

<ProblemReports ticket={4471} problem="parser" />

### `hint`: the booking-v3 form tells people to type month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. The tenant's people do what it says and type
month first; the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first. The month flips the same way,
so the body of the report looks like `parser`, but the parser fix does not touch it: the fix is
the hint on the form. Only form reports from booking-v3 tenants have it.

<ProblemReports ticket={4471} problem="hint" />

### `one-day`: the date lands one day early

Nothing is flipped in these. A booking moves to the day before ("Tuesday 12 May" shows as Monday
11 May), the same meeting shows on different days in Lisbon and Toronto, an invite arrives
before the booking was made, and one tenant sees it only in June. That is a time zone
or daylight-saving conversion, not date order. The desk had noted these, then counted them under
`parser` by tenant, so they would stay open after the parser fix ships.

<ProblemReports ticket={4471} problem="one-day" />

## Why intake's tag does not decide it

Intake guesses the tag from the reporter's browser locale. The problem depends on the tenant:
its locale and its booking form (`#lib/kb/locales.ts`). The "intake tagged" column shows where
the two disagree.

## What changes for the desk

- Close #4471 against the parser fix only. Track `hint` and `one-day` in their own tickets.
- A shared subject line is not a shared cause. Before merging into a big ticket, check the
  tenant, its form and the channel (`causeOf` in `#lib/kb/causes.ts`), and read the body for a
  one-day move.

<RenderStamp {signature} />
